# CLAUDE.md

Instructions for Claude Code. Apply these preferences in every session, across all projects.

## Git / commits

- Never commit code, ever. Always wait for explicit user approval first. Only run `git commit` (or push) after the user has confirmed, even for small or already-discussed changes.
- Write commit messages and PR titles in [Conventional Commits 1.0.0](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/) format: `<type>[optional scope][!]: <description>`.
- Never sign commits or PR descriptions with Claude:
  - No `Co-Authored-By: Claude ... <noreply@anthropic.com>` trailer on commits.
  - No `Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/...` line on commits.
  - No "Generated with Claude Code" footer or session URL in PR bodies or issue text.
- Never sign commits or PR descriptions with Cursor:
  - No `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>` trailer on commits.
  - No similar Cursor co-author trailers, attribution footers, or session URLs in commits, PR bodies, or issue text.

## Code comments

- Comments must describe only the final resource/code as it stands. Never narrate historical changes ("no longer", "used to", "instead of", "was removed", "still", "NOT deleted", "kept"). If something changes, rewrite the comment to describe the new state directly, don't diff it against the old one.

## Markdown formatting

- Never hard-wrap text inside a paragraph (inserting a newline at ~70-80 chars like typed prose). Write each paragraph as a single line, regardless of length. This applies to PR/commit descriptions, Slack and Teams messages, issue comments, and any other markdown that gets rendered. GitHub and Slack render single newlines as visible line breaks, so hard-wrapping shows up as broken mid-sentence lines instead of a clean paragraph.
- Only break lines for actual structure: separate paragraphs (blank line between them), list items, headers, code blocks, etc. A message with several distinct points needs that structure, not one long paragraph.

## Writing style

- Never use em dashes (—) in prose. Use commas, colons, or periods instead, the way people write day to day.
- For plans, reports, and user-facing messages, run them through `ste-writing` (ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English discipline plus its anti-slop linter) and `no-ai-slop` before finalizing.
- For text an agent or tool parses with no human to resolve ambiguity (tool and skill descriptions, error messages, system prompts, subagent prompts, inter-agent instructions, status reports), use `asd-ste100`. Ask for strict mode on procedures, error messages, and tool descriptions. Ask for STE-flavored mode on READMEs, PR descriptions, and explanatory prose. Ask it to "show the diff" to see each broken rule. Its linter runs as `python ~/.claude/skills/asd-ste100/scripts/ste-lint.py <file>`. It never drops a fact or condition to shorten a sentence. Do not use it for creative or marketing copy.

## Personality and tone

Three non-competing skills, each at a different layer. Use all three in chat replies:

- `caveman` (language layer): strip filler, polite fluff, and unnecessary prose from the chat reply itself. Code is untouched. This only shrinks what has to be read, not what gets written.
- `ponytail` (architecture/code layer): take the laziest, most direct path. Before adding code or a dependency, ask whether it needs to exist at all. Prefer built-in platform features and native tools over new packages. Fewer lines, fewer tokens spent executing.
- `i-have-adhd` (structure layer): lead with the answer or action item, not a wind-up. Don't bury the solution at the end of an explanation. For open-ended problems, brainstorm via independent parallel threads rather than one linear chain.

Scope: chat replies only. Persisted artifacts (commits, PRs, issues, docs, memory files, Slack and Teams messages) stay in normal prose with whatever tone that artifact's own section specifies (e.g. Slack and Teams messages below). Don't carry caveman's compression into those.

## Terraform

- Never upgrade or install Terraform via brew. Always set the Terraform version with [tenv](https://github.com/tofuutils/tenv) (`tenv terraform install <version>`), and run terraform via the tenv-managed binary so it matches each project's `required_version` constraint.
- For AWS resources, primarily use registry modules from [terraform-aws-modules](https://github.com/terraform-aws-modules) and [aws-ia](https://github.com/aws-ia) instead of hand-writing raw resources, whenever a suitable module exists.
- Three distinct Terraform tools, pick by task: `terraform-generator` to write new `.tf`/HCL (see DevOps skills below), `terraform-validator` to lint/audit it (tflint, checkov), and `terraform` (MCP) for plan previews, state inspection, module docs, or drift detection on code that already exists.
- Same split for Terragrunt: `terragrunt-skill` for architecture (stacks, units, catalog patterns, CI/CD pipelines, AWS/GCP OIDC, multi-account layouts); `terragrunt-generator`/`terragrunt-validator` for quick single-file HCL generation/linting (see DevOps skills below).

## Skill triggers

- After writing or modifying code, use `code-simplifier` before declaring the task done.
- For AWS IAM, Secrets Manager, CloudFormation/CDK, containers, or observability work, use `aws-core`.
- For up-to-date library/API documentation instead of relying on training-cutoff knowledge, use `context7`.
- For writing or pressure-testing documentation against the actual code, use `grill-with-docs`.
- For codebase structure, dependency, or "where does X connect" questions, run `/graphify` once to build the graph, then use `graphify query` before broad file searches.
- When debugging, use `superpowers:systematic-debugging`; before declaring a task done, use `superpowers:verification-before-completion`; for planning complex work, use `superpowers:writing-plans`.
- For ML-training-focused work, use `andrej-karpathy-skills`.
- For Kubernetes manifests/Helm/Kustomize/cluster policies, use `kubernetes-skill` when the work is security/reliability-sensitive (insecure defaults, resource starvation, network exposure, privilege sprawl, fragile rollouts, API drift); use `k8s-yaml-generator`/`k8s-yaml-validator` (see DevOps skills below) for quick, low-stakes manifest scaffolding/linting instead.

## DevOps skills (`devops-skills@akin-ozer`)

Most domains have a `<domain>-generator` (write/scaffold new files) and a `<domain>-validator` (lint/audit/fix existing ones). Use the generator when creating, the validator when checking. Domains: `ansible`, `azure-pipelines`, `bash-script` (ShellCheck), `dockerfile`, `fluentbit`, `github-actions`, `gitlab-ci`, `helm`, `jenkinsfile`, `k8s-yaml`, `makefile`, `promql`, `terraform` (tflint, checkov), `terragrunt` (single-file `.hcl`).

- Generate-only, no validator: `logql-generator` (Grafana Loki LogQL) and `loki-config-generator` (Loki server configs).
- Standalone: `k8s-debug` diagnoses a *running* cluster via `kubectl`. For manifest files, not live state, use `k8s-yaml-generator`/`k8s-yaml-validator`.

## Manual-install skills (not `/plugin`-installable)

These ship as bare `SKILL.md` folders, not plugin marketplaces (no `.claude-plugin/marketplace.json`). The base role clones each `ai.manual_skills` entry into `~/.claude/skills/<name>/`, links it into `~/.cursor/skills/<name>/`, and runs `npx apex-skills` for `aws-samples/sample-apex-skills`. Each skill's own description says when to use it. Routing notes:

- `eks-operation-review` audits the current health of a live EKS cluster. `eks-upgrade-check` scores upgrade readiness. Use the first for "how is the cluster", the second for "about to upgrade".
- `k8s-debug` (devops-skills) first for a quick, broad look. Use `k8s-dns-troubleshooting`, `k8s-network-troubleshooting`, or `k8s-namespace-troubleshooting` when the problem is already DNS-, network-, or namespace-scoped, or `k8s-debug` didn't resolve it.
- `aws-well-architected-review` exists twice: the manual copy and the one in `aws-core@claude-plugins-official`. Prefer the `aws-core` one.
- `eks-resilience-checker` output feeds `chaos-engineering-on-aws`.
- `security-audit` is clone-only. It is not the official `security-guidance` plugin.
- `no-ai-slop` pairs with `ste-writing` (see Writing style above).

Manual skills by source:

- `mattpocock/skills`: `grill-with-docs`.
- `aws-samples/sample-apex-skills`: `apex-skills` (25+ AWS platform-engineering skills, own npx installer).
